Step 1: Clone the Repository
First, you need a local copy of your code on your computer. You only need to do this step one time. If you already have a local copy, you can skip to Step 2.
Action:
1.	Open your terminal.
2.	Navigate to a directory where you want to store your projects (e.g., cd ~/Documents/Projects).
3.	Clone the repository from GitHub. This command copies the entire project and its history to your machine.
Bash
git clone https://github.com/MaginnGroup/Cassandra.git

Step 2: Enter the Project Directory
All git commands need to be run from inside the project folder.
Action:
1.	Move into the new directory that git just created:
Bash
cd Cassandra

Step 3: Create and Switch to Your New Branch
This is the most important step. You are creating a new, parallel timeline called modernization. Your main timeline, master, will be untouched.
The command git checkout -b <branch-name> is a shortcut that does two things:
•	git branch modernization: Creates a new branch.
•	git checkout modernization: Switches you to that new branch.
Action:
1.	Run this command:
Bash
git checkout -b modernization
You'll see a message like Switched to a new branch 'modernization'.

Step 4: Verify Your Location (Optional but Recommended)
Let's just confirm you are in the right place.
Action:
1.	Run this command to see all your local branches:
Bash
git branch
You will see a list of branches, and your active one will have an asterisk * next to it:
* modernization
  master
This confirms you are safely on the modernization branch.
Summary: You Are Ready!
That's it. You are now in your sandbox.
Any changes you make, any files you add, and any code you write will be saved only to the modernization branch. Your master branch remains clean, safe, and untouched.
Your next step is to start Phase 2 by creating a new python/ folder inside this Cassandra directory and adding your first file, api.py.
